<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>critical-thinking</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>critical-thinking</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Critical Thinking</li><li>Literary Analysis and Interpretation</li></ul><h2>Critical Thinking</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Critical Thinking — Critical Thinking, English (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: critical thinking · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Critical Thinking</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat &quot;Critical Thinking&quot; as one blob → ✅ split into: critical thinking.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Critical Thinking (Critical Thinking, English, class-11-notes) covers Critical Thinking. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Literary Analysis and Interpretation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Literary Analysis and Interpretation — Critical Thinking, English (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: literary analysis, interpretation · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Interpretation.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Literary Analysis</td><